WEBVTT

NOTE SE201 Lesson 3: Decompose the system into a graph — captions generated from the narration script

1
00:00:00.500 --> 00:00:03.989
Software engineering 201. Lesson three.

2
00:00:03.989 --> 00:00:07.030
Decompose the system into a graph.

3
00:00:07.380 --> 00:00:09.989
Quick question. An agent hands you a plan.

4
00:00:09.989 --> 00:00:11.915
Ten steps, numbered one to ten.

5
00:00:11.915 --> 00:00:15.829
How many of those steps truly have to wait for the step before?

6
00:00:15.829 --> 00:00:19.743
All ten? About half? Or is it impossible to tell from the list?

7
00:00:19.743 --> 00:00:24.464
You can't tell. A numbered list records the order someone wrote things down.

8
00:00:24.464 --> 00:00:26.390
It doesn't say what needs what.

9
00:00:26.750 --> 00:00:31.907
So here's the test. Take any two steps, and ask: what crosses between them?

10
00:00:31.907 --> 00:00:36.514
Think about cooking dinner. The sauce waits for the chopped onions.

11
00:00:36.514 --> 00:00:39.745
Something crosses, so that's a real dependency.

12
00:00:39.745 --> 00:00:42.427
Draw the arrow. The rice and the salad?

13
00:00:42.427 --> 00:00:47.377
Nothing crosses. So nobody waits for the rice before starting the salad.

14
00:00:47.377 --> 00:00:49.440
And then, is not a dependency.

15
00:00:49.780 --> 00:00:51.760
In 101, the unit was the loop.

16
00:00:51.760 --> 00:00:55.323
Plan, build, verify, until one piece of work is right.

17
00:00:55.323 --> 00:00:57.170
The graph sits one level up.

18
00:00:57.170 --> 00:01:02.713
It says which pieces exist, what each one needs, and which can run at the same time.

19
00:01:02.713 --> 00:01:04.825
And it doesn't replace the loop.

20
00:01:04.825 --> 00:01:08.190
Every node in the graph is still a loop of its own.

21
00:01:08.550 --> 00:01:11.975
Let's decompose a real system: the site you're on.

22
00:01:11.975 --> 00:01:14.030
Start with the parts. AI tips.

23
00:01:14.030 --> 00:01:16.085
Software engineering 101. 201.

24
00:01:16.085 --> 00:01:20.332
And the book. Each part has its own folder, and its own pages.

25
00:01:20.332 --> 00:01:23.757
A new lesson in one needs nothing from the others.

26
00:01:23.757 --> 00:01:26.360
So work on one never waits on another.

27
00:01:26.710 --> 00:01:28.596
But they aren't floating free.

28
00:01:28.596 --> 00:01:32.179
All four are built by one generator, with one stylesheet.

29
00:01:32.179 --> 00:01:36.076
That shared piece is a node too, and every part depends on it.

30
00:01:36.076 --> 00:01:38.779
So the graph shows you where to be careful.

31
00:01:38.779 --> 00:01:41.041
Change one part, and one part moves.

32
00:01:41.041 --> 00:01:43.870
Change the shared node, and everything moves.

33
00:01:43.870 --> 00:01:46.950
Which is why a shared node needs one clear owner.

34
00:01:47.300 --> 00:01:49.943
Now zoom into one node: a single video lesson.

35
00:01:49.943 --> 00:01:53.564
Here, the arrows are real. The narration clips need the script.

36
00:01:53.564 --> 00:01:58.046
The render needs those clips, because the length of the audio sets the timing.

37
00:01:58.046 --> 00:02:00.862
Captions need the timeline the render writes out.

38
00:02:00.862 --> 00:02:06.436
And the page needs all of it, before a pull request to staging, where only I merge to production.

39
00:02:06.436 --> 00:02:08.160
Every arrow carries something.

40
00:02:08.510 --> 00:02:11.028
Script, narration, render. A strict chain.

41
00:02:11.028 --> 00:02:15.224
More agents won't make it shorter: the longest chain sets the minimum.

42
00:02:15.224 --> 00:02:17.082
But after the render, it forks.

43
00:02:17.082 --> 00:02:19.000
Captions need only the timeline.

44
00:02:19.000 --> 00:02:22.058
The sound level and the poster need only the video.

45
00:02:22.058 --> 00:02:24.096
Nothing crosses between those two.

46
00:02:24.096 --> 00:02:27.513
The build script happens to run them one after the other.

47
00:02:27.513 --> 00:02:30.570
The graph shows that's a choice, not a requirement.

48
00:02:30.920 --> 00:02:32.875
Why does this matter? Waiting.

49
00:02:32.875 --> 00:02:36.525
Picture three parts, with nothing crossing between them.

50
00:02:36.525 --> 00:02:39.914
Run as a numbered list, each one waits for the last.

51
00:02:39.914 --> 00:02:42.325
Run as a graph, they go side by side.

52
00:02:42.325 --> 00:02:44.150
Same work. Far less waiting.

53
00:02:44.500 --> 00:02:47.442
Now scale that up. Picture an enterprise system.

54
00:02:47.442 --> 00:02:49.588
Orders, billing, shipping, support.

55
00:02:49.588 --> 00:02:52.530
Far too big for one loop. It's the same picture.

56
00:02:52.530 --> 00:02:56.882
A few real arrows: billing needs the order, shipping needs the payment.

57
00:02:56.882 --> 00:03:00.192
A shared node everything leans on, like customer data.

58
00:03:00.192 --> 00:03:04.606
And parts that never touch, where teams and agents move without waiting.

59
00:03:04.606 --> 00:03:08.100
Miss the shared node, and two of them collide at the end.

60
00:03:08.450 --> 00:03:11.663
So, to recap. Drawing the graph is four moves.

61
00:03:11.663 --> 00:03:15.504
One. Name the parts, each with a boundary and an owner.

62
00:03:15.504 --> 00:03:19.625
Two. For every pair, ask what crosses, and what they share.

63
00:03:19.625 --> 00:03:21.720
Three. Draw only those arrows.

64
00:03:21.720 --> 00:03:26.260
And four. Give every part its own finish line, and its own check.

65
00:03:26.620 --> 00:03:29.279
Which gives us rule three, for your agents.

66
00:03:29.279 --> 00:03:32.681
Before any job with more than one part, draw the graph.

67
00:03:32.681 --> 00:03:37.876
Name the parts. Draw an arrow only where something crosses from one part to another.

68
00:03:37.876 --> 00:03:41.711
Anything two parts share becomes its own node, with one owner.

69
00:03:41.711 --> 00:03:45.050
Parts with no arrow between them run at the same time.

70
00:03:45.050 --> 00:03:48.390
And every part gets its own loop: plan, build, verify.

71
00:03:48.740 --> 00:03:53.486
Now, your turn. Take the last multi-step plan an agent gave you.

72
00:03:53.486 --> 00:03:56.230
At every, and then, ask what crosses.

73
00:03:56.230 --> 00:03:58.010
How many arrows survive?

74
00:03:58.360 --> 00:04:04.100
Next time, those parts really do run at once, and we bring the work back together.

75
00:04:04.100 --> 00:04:05.710
See you in lesson four.
